<h2>3. Line-by-Line Code Breakdown & Customization Guide</h2>

<h3>3.1 <code>desktop_app.py</code> (The Application Launcher)</h3>
<p>This is the first file that runs when the user double-clicks the application.</p>

<pre><code># Lines 1-8: Importing required tools
import os             # Works with computer files and folders
import sys            # Accesses Python runtime settings and command line
import threading      # Allows running multiple tasks at the exact same time
import time           # Handles delays, timestamps, and timeouts
import traceback      # Captures full error messages if something crashes
import urllib.request # Performs internal health check calls to the local server
import webview        # Creates the native Microsoft Edge desktop window
import uvicorn        # The fast web server engine that runs our backend API</code></pre>

<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> These standard libraries give Python the power to interact with the Windows OS, run parallel tasks, and draw a window.</p>

<pre><code># Line 14: Granting automatic microphone permissions
os.environ["WEBVIEW2_ADDITIONAL_BROWSER_ARGUMENTS"] = "--use-fake-ui-for-media-stream --enable-media-stream"</code></pre>
<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> When a browser accesses a microphone, it usually pops up an annoying <em>"Do you want to allow this app to use your microphone?"</em> prompt. This line tells the Edge Chromium engine: <em>"Always auto-allow the helmet microphone without asking the operator every time."</em></p>

<pre><code># Lines 69-76: Starting the Backend Web Engine
def start_backend_server():
    uvicorn.run(app, host="127.0.0.1", port=8765, log_level="error")</code></pre>
<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Starts the local API server on <code>127.0.0.1</code> (your local PC) on port <code>8765</code>.</p>

<pre><code># Lines 137-144: Opening the Desktop Window
window = webview.create_window(
    title="Studds QC Testing Dashboard",
    url="http://127.0.0.1:8765/",
    width=1100,
    height=800,
    resizable=True,
    js_api=desktop_api
)
webview.start()</code></pre>
<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Opens an 1100x800 pixel desktop window titled <em>"Studds QC Testing Dashboard"</em> showing our inspection screen.</p>

<hr>

<h3>3.2 <code>db_config.json</code> (Database Settings File)</h3>
<p>This file sits directly beside the application executable. You can open and edit it in <strong>Notepad</strong> without writing any code.</p>

<pre><code>{
    "host": "localhost",
    "port": 3306,
    "user": "root",
    "password": "",
    "database": "studds_qc",
    "connection_timeout": 8
}</code></pre>

<table>
    <tr>
        <th>Key</th>
        <th>Default Value</th>
        <th>Plain English Meaning</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>"host"</code></td>
        <td><code>"localhost"</code></td>
        <td>The IP address of the MySQL server. Use <code>"localhost"</code> if on the same PC, or an IP like <code>"192.168.1.100"</code> for a central factory server.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>"port"</code></td>
        <td><code>3306</code></td>
        <td>The standard MySQL networking port.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>"user"</code></td>
        <td><code>"root"</code></td>
        <td>The database username.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>"password"</code></td>
        <td><code>""</code></td>
        <td>The database login password.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>"database"</code></td>
        <td><code>"studds_qc"</code></td>
        <td>The database table catalog name.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>"connection_timeout"</code></td>
        <td><code>8</code></td>
        <td>Seconds to wait before warning if the database server is offline.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<hr>

<h3>3.3 <code>speaker_test.py</code> (Stereo Speaker Player)</h3>
<p>Uses Windows native Multimedia commands (<code>winmm.dll</code>) to play stereo sound cues without opening any external media player.</p>

<pre><code># Lines 22-31: Short-Path Converter
def get_short_path_name(long_name: str) -> str:
    ...
    res = kernel32.GetShortPathNameW(long_name, buf, 260)</code></pre>
<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Windows sometimes fails to play audio if folder names have spaces (like <code>C:\Program Files\Studds</code>). This converts paths to 8.3 short format (<code>C:\PROGRA~1\Studds</code>) so playback never fails.</p>

<pre><code># Lines 123-135: Playing the Stereo Audio
def start_speaker_test(mp3_path: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    ...
    _mci_send(f'open "{short_path}" type mpegvideo alias speaker_mp3')
    _mci_send("play speaker_mp3")</code></pre>
<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Opens the MP3 audio file and starts playback. The audio plays sound strictly in the <strong>Left Channel</strong> first, pauses, and then plays strictly in the <strong>Right Channel</strong>.</p>

<hr>

<h3>3.4 <code>mic_test.py</code> (Offline Speech Recognition Engine)</h3>
<p>Uses the <strong>Vosk Offline Neural Speech Model</strong> to transcribe what the operator says into the microphone without needing any internet connection.</p>

<pre><code># Lines 94-96: Loading the Neural Speech Model
VOSK_MODEL_PATH = os.path.join(get_bundle_dir(), "vosk-model")
speech_model = vosk.Model(VOSK_MODEL_PATH)</code></pre>
<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Loads the offline acoustic speech dictionary from the <code>vosk-model</code> folder into memory.</p>

<pre><code># Lines 146-153: Checking for Target Voice Words
target_phrases = ["hello studds", "studds", "testing", "hello"]
passed = any(phrase in transcript for phrase in target_phrases)

return {
    "status": "PASS" if passed else "FAIL",
    "transcript": transcript
}</code></pre>
<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> When the operator speaks into the helmet mic, Vosk transcribes the audio. If the operator says <em>"hello studds"</em>, <em>"studds"</em>, <em>"testing"</em>, or <em>"hello"</em>, the test automatically <span class="badge-pass">PASSES</span>.</p>

<hr>

<h3>3.5 <code>button_detector.py</code> (Physical Button Sensor)</h3>
<p>Listens for physical button presses on the helmet headset: Volume Up, Volume Down, and Play/Pause.</p>

<pre><code># Lines 64-66: Key Code Definitions
VK_VOLUME_DOWN = 0xAE        # Hardware key code for Volume Down
VK_VOLUME_UP = 0xAF          # Hardware key code for Volume Up
VK_MEDIA_PLAY_PAUSE = 0xB3   # Hardware key code for Play/Pause</code></pre>

<pre><code># Lines 203-214: Volume Change Detection
current_volume = volume_ctrl.GetMasterVolumeLevelScalar()
if diff > 0.005:
    button_state["volume_up"] = True
    print("Volume Up detected")
elif diff < -0.005:
    button_state["volume_down"] = True
    print("Volume Down detected")</code></pre>
<p><strong>Explanation:</strong> Measures the volume level in real time. If the operator presses Volume (+), the volume level rises and turns on the green <strong>Volume Up</strong> badge on screen. If they press Volume (-), it turns on the green <strong>Volume Down</strong> badge.</p>

<hr>

<h3>3.6 <code>qc_bluetooth_api.py</code> (Backend API & Database Router)</h3>
<p>Provides all web endpoints that connect the user interface to the hardware and database.</p>

<table>
    <tr>
        <th>Endpoint</th>
        <th>Method</th>
        <th>What It Does (Plain English)</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>/health</code></td>
        <td><code>GET</code></td>
        <td>Quickly replies <code>{"status": "healthy"}</code> so the launcher knows the backend is ready.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>/scan</code></td>
        <td><code>GET</code></td>
        <td>Starts Bluetooth scanning and returns a list of nearby helmets with signal strength (RSSI).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>/test/{address}</code></td>
        <td><code>POST</code></td>
        <td>Automatically pairs with the selected helmet and checks audio/mic profiles.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>/analyze_mic</code></td>
        <td><code>POST</code></td>
        <td>Receives recorded audio from the mic, runs speech recognition, and returns the transcript.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>/speaker_test/start</code></td>
        <td><code>POST</code></td>
        <td>Starts playing the Left/Right stereo audio file into the helmet.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>/button_test/status</code></td>
        <td><code>GET</code></td>
        <td>Returns whether Volume Up, Down, or Play buttons were pressed (true/false).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>/check_duplicate/{mac}</code></td>
        <td><code>GET</code></td>
        <td>Queries MySQL to check if this helmet was already tested today.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>/save_report</code></td>
        <td><code>POST</code></td>
        <td>Saves the complete inspection record (Operator ID, Serial Number, MAC, PASS/FAIL) to MySQL.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code>/reports</code></td>
        <td><code>GET</code></td>
        <td>Fetches past inspection logs for the table at the bottom of the screen.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>4. Step-by-Step Recipes: How to Change Common Settings</h2>

<div class="recipe-box">
    <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: #15803d;">Recipe 1: How to Change the Database IP / Password</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Open the folder where the app is installed.</li>
        <li>Locate the file <code>db_config.json</code>.</li>
        <li>Right-click it and select <strong>Open with &rarr; Notepad</strong>.</li>
        <li>Change the <code>"host"</code> and <code>"password"</code> values:
<pre><code>{
    "host": "192.168.1.50",
    "port": 3306,
    "user": "qc_user",
    "password": "NewSecurePassword2026",
    "database": "studds_qc",
    "connection_timeout": 8
}</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>Click <strong>File &rarr; Save</strong> and restart the application. That's it!</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<div class="recipe-box">
    <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: #15803d;">Recipe 2: How to Change the Voice Words the Helmet Listens For</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Open <code>01_Source_Code\mic_test.py</code> in Notepad or VS Code.</li>
        <li>Go to <strong>Line 147</strong>.</li>
        <li>Find this line:
<pre><code>target_phrases = ["hello studds", "studds", "testing", "hello"]</code></pre></li>
        <li>Add your new words in quotes:
<pre><code>target_phrases = ["hello studds", "studds", "testing", "hello", "motorcycle", "pass"]</code></pre></li>
        <li>Save the file.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<div class="recipe-box">
    <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: #15803d;">Recipe 3: How to Change the Application Window Title or Size</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Open <code>01_Source_Code\desktop_app.py</code> in Notepad.</li>
        <li>Go to <strong>Lines 138–142</strong>:
<pre><code>window = webview.create_window(
    title="Studds QC Testing Dashboard",
    url="http://127.0.0.1:8765/",
    width=1100,
    height=800,
    resizable=True,
    js_api=desktop_api
)</code></pre></li>
        <li>Change <code>title="..."</code> to your preferred title.</li>
        <li>Change <code>width=1100</code> and <code>height=800</code> to your desired size (e.g., <code>1280</code> x <code>900</code>).</li>
        <li>Save the file.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<h2>5. How to Rebuild & Create a New <code>.exe</code> Installer</h2>

<p>Whenever you modify any Python or HTML code, you can easily generate a fresh standalone <code>.exe</code> and setup installer using simple double-clicks:</p>

<div class="recipe-box">
    <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: #15803d;">Method 1: The 1-Click Direct Double-Click (Easiest Method)</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Step 1 — Create the <code>.exe</code>:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Open the <code>01_Source_Code</code> folder in File Explorer.</li>
                <li><strong>Directly double-click on <code>build.bat</code></strong>.</li>
                <li>A black window will open and automatically compile everything. Wait 1–2 minutes until it completes and creates <code>01_Source_Code\dist\StuddsQC.exe</code>.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Step 2 — Create the Setup Installer:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>In the same <code>01_Source_Code</code> folder, <strong>directly double-click on <code>studds_installer.iss</code></strong>.</li>
                <li>It will open in Inno Setup Compiler. Click the green <strong>Run</strong> / <strong>Compile</strong> button at the top (or press <code>Ctrl + F9</code>).</li>
                <li>Inno Setup will output <code>01_Source_Code\installer_output\Studds_QC_Setup_v1.0.exe</code>. Double-click this setup file to install on any laptop!</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<div class="header-box" style="margin-top: 10pt;">
    <h4 style="margin-top: 0; color: #1e40af;">Method 2: Via Command Prompt (Alternative)</h4>
    <ol style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        <li>Open the folder <code>01_Source_Code</code>.</li>
        <li>Click in the top address bar, type <strong><code>cmd</code></strong>, and press <strong>Enter</strong>.</li>
        <li>Type <code>build.bat</code> and press <strong>Enter</strong> to compile.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<h2>6. Troubleshooting & Emergency Fixes</h2>

<table>
    <tr>
        <th style="width: 25%;">Problem Observed</th>
        <th style="width: 35%;">Plain English Cause</th>
        <th style="width: 40%;">Easy Fix</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>"Backend Did Not Start" error box</strong></td>
        <td>Port 8765 is blocked by another program, or Python crashed.</td>
        <td>1. Open Task Manager and close any duplicate <code>StuddsQC.exe</code> processes.<br>2. Open <code>startup_error.log</code> in Notepad to see the exact crash line.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Top bar shows 🔴 Database Offline</strong></td>
        <td><code>db_config.json</code> has the wrong password or MySQL is stopped.</td>
        <td>1. Ensure XAMPP or MySQL server is turned ON (running).<br>2. Open <code>db_config.json</code> and check the IP and password.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Microphone test always says "FAIL"</strong></td>
        <td>Operator spoke too quietly or model folder was missing.</td>
        <td>1. Speak clearly within 5 cm of the microphone.<br>2. Verify the <code>vosk-model</code> folder exists inside the installation directory.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><strong>Bluetooth helmet won't appear in list</strong></td>
        <td>Helmet is not in pairing mode or Bluetooth is switched OFF.</td>
        <td>1. Hold helmet power button for 5 seconds until LED flashes red/blue.<br>2. Ensure Windows Bluetooth is turned ON in Settings.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

